  "mood": "Madhyamavati has a calm, auspicious, and uplifting character. It is strongly associated with devotional closure, so it often leaves the listener with a sense of peace, completion, and blessing. The raga feels spacious and balanced because of its even note spacing and pentatonic simplicity, making it suitable for elaboration without tension. It evokes images of serenity, prayer, and a dignified festive calm, and is especially apt for the concluding moments of a concert or for music meant to feel propitious and reassuring.",
  "description": "Madhyamavati is a janya rāga of the 22nd melakarta Kharaharapriya, though the text notes that it can also be derived from Charukesi, Natabhairavi, or Harikambhoji by omitting both gandharam and dhaivatam. It is an audava (pentatonic) rāga with a symmetric ascent and descent, using only S, R2, M1, P, and N2. The scale is described as especially auspicious, and in Carnatic concert practice it is commonly used to conclude performances, either as the final song or as the rāga in which the ending of the last song is rendered. The raga is also noted for being suitable for elaboration and exploration because of its even spacing of notes. Its notes correspond to the first three notes of the cycle of fifths (S, P, R2) and fourths (S, M1, N2).",
  "time_of_day": "",
  "gamaka_usage": "The source does not give detailed gamaka prescriptions, but the raga’s pentatonic symmetry and even spacing suggest smooth, restrained ornamentation rather than heavy oscillation. R2 is typically treated as a gently ornamented note, M1 and P are usually held steady as structural pillars, and N2 may receive light kampita or a soft slide into upper S. Because the raga lacks gandharam and dhaivatam, phrases should remain clean and direct, with jaru-like transitions between the available notes rather than dense gamaka clusters. Compared with closely related ragas such as Manirangu or Kedaragaula, Madhyamavati is more straightforward and less colored by extra descending notes.",
